Package: DisentFunSpaces
Type: Package
Title: Build and Compare Functional Spaces from Mixed-Type Trait Data
Version: 0.8.1
Authors@R: c(
    person("Aurèle", "Toussaint", email = "aurele.toussaint@cnrs.fr",
           role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("Pablo", "Tedesco", role = "ctb"),
    person("Gaël", "Grenouillet", role = "ctb"),
    person("Liis", "Kasari-Toussaint", role = "ctb"),
    person("Sébastien", "Brosse", role = "ctb"))
Description: Generalises the framework of Toussaint et al. (2026)
    <doi:10.1002/ecog.08578> to any trait dataset. Traits are described by a
    type (continuous, ordinal, nominal, binary, multichoice, fuzzy-coded,
    circular) and assigned to functional dimensions (e.g. locomotion, diet,
    reproduction). A functional space is built for each dimension and for any
    combination of dimensions from a generalised Gower distance (Pavoine et
    al. (2009) <doi:10.1111/j.1600-0706.2008.16668.x>) followed by a Principal
    Coordinates Analysis. Spaces are compared with Procrustes tests, and
    species-level functional uniqueness (FUn), specialisation (FSp) and FUSE
    are computed in each space. Groups of species defined by any categorical
    attribute that is not a trait (origin, human uses, migration, threat
    status...) are compared in each space (convex hull overlap and functional
    beta diversity, Villeger et al. (2013) <doi:10.1111/geb.12021>; PERMANOVA),
    and the species of focal groups are positioned relative to a reference
    group (e.g. non-native relative to native species: functional novelty,
    loss of uniqueness, change in functional richness with null models). A
    'shiny' application exposes the full workflow, and the whole analysis can
    be exported as a reproducible project (data, numbered scripts, parameters,
    results and a Methods and Results report). Example data: observed traits
    of 2000 bird species, described by the traits of Toussaint et al. (2026),
    from AVONET (Tobias et al. (2022) <doi:10.1111/ele.13898>), EltonTraits
    (Wilman et al. (2014) <doi:10.1890/13-1917.1>) and the Amniote
    life-history database (Myhrvold et al. (2015) <doi:10.1890/15-0846R.1>).
